            "body": "Blocks the cheapest way to build an AFDB catalog. Tracking here so it gets re-tested when upstream moves; nothing to fix in this repo.\n\n## What happens\n\nEvery `AF-*.cif` at the root of `gs://public-datasets-deepmind-alphafold-v4` is stored with `Content-Encoding: gzip`. GCS transcodes on read, omits `Content-Length`, and the Rust `object_store` crate treats that as fatal:\n\n```\nGeneric GCS error: Content-Length Header missing from response\n```\n\nBoth obstore and icechunk are built on `object_store`, so this is not only an ingest problem. A virtual chunk that *points* at one of these objects fails at **read** time with the identical error \u2014 confirmed end to end against a real icechunk repository:\n\n```\nerror fetching virtual reference\n|-> Generic GCS error: Content-Length Header missing from response\n```\n\nA manifest over the flat objects would therefore be unreadable, not merely slow to build.\n\n## Why the documented workaround does not rescue it\n\nTwo upstream issues compose:\n\n- [apache/arrow-rs-object-store#774](https://github.com/apache/arrow-rs-object-store/issues/774) \u2014 transcoding drops `Content-Length`. Sending `Accept-Encoding: gzip` avoids transcoding, but only below ~8 MiB stored.\n- [apache/arrow-rs-object-store#783](https://github.com/apache/arrow-rs-object-store/issues/783) \u2014 that header is applied to the OAuth token request too, which then fails.\n\nAFDB objects are 60-95 kB stored, so #774's size limit does not bite and the workaround ought to apply. It does not. Passing `bearer_token` avoids the token fetch #783 breaks, and the read still fails:\n\n```python\nstore = GCSStore(BUCKET, bearer_token=tok,\n                 client_options={\"default_headers\": {\"accept-encoding\": \"gzip\"}})\nobstore.get_range(store, \"AF-A0A7C3LD06-F1-model_v4.cif\", start=0, end=16)\n# Generic GCS error: Content-Length Header missing from response\n```\n\nSupplying credentials is not a way around this.\n\n## The server is fine\n\nRaw HTTP with the same header against the same object:\n\n```\nHTTP/2 206\ncontent-range: bytes 0-15/95204\ncontent-encoding: gzip\ncontent-length: 16\n```\n\nBody starts `1f8b`. So GCS honours the range and sends a `Content-Length`; the header is either not reaching the request or not having its intended effect inside `object_store`.\n\n## What it costs us\n\nFrom `docs/afdb-catalog-cost.md`: addressing the flat objects directly is **$7** for MarinFold's 4.21M entries and finishes in under an hour. The proteome-tar path that works today is **$273-$477**. This issue is worth roughly a fortieth of the build cost.\n\n## What to do\n\nNothing here for now \u2014 the catalog is built from `proteomes/*.tar`, which are `identity` encoding with clean ranged reads. Re-test the three-line reproducer above whenever `object_store` releases; if it passes, the flat-object path becomes available and is dramatically cheaper.\n\nUpstream also has [#806](https://github.com/apache/arrow-rs-object-store/issues/806) (200 response to a whole-object range request), which is adjacent but not what bites here.\n\nVersions tested: obstore 0.11.0, icechunk 2.1.2.",
